Task 1: Configure IdM DNS Integration
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current DNS Configuration
First, let's examine the current DNS configuration in our IdM environment.

Connect to the IdM server
# SSH to the IdM server (if not already connected)
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
Authenticate with IdM admin credentials
# Obtain Kerberos ticket for admin user
kinit admin
# Enter the admin password when prompted
Check current DNS configuration
# Display current DNS configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show

# Check DNS zones
ipa dnszone-find
Verify DNS service status
# Check if DNS service is running
systemctl status named-pkcs11

# Check IdM services status
ipactl status
Subtask 1.2: Configure DNS Forwarders
DNS forwarders are essential for resolving external domain names. Let's configure them properly.

Add DNS forwarders using ipa dnsconfig-mod
# Add Google DNS and Cloudflare DNS as forwarders
ipa dnsconfig-mod --forwarder=8.8.8.8 --forwarder=1.1.1.1

# Verify the configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show
Configure forward policy
# Set forward policy to 'first' (try forwarders first, then local resolution)
ipa dnsconfig-mod --forward-policy=first

# Alternative: Set to 'only' to use only forwarders
# ipa dnsconfig-mod --forward-policy=only
Test external DNS resolution
# Test resolution of external domains
dig @localhost google.com
dig @localhost redhat.com

# Test using nslookup
nslookup google.com localhost
Subtask 1.3: Configure Reverse DNS Zones
Reverse DNS zones are crucial for proper IdM functionality and security.

Create reverse DNS zone for the lab network
# Create reverse zone for 192.168.1.0/24 network
ipa dnszone-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa.

# Verify the zone was created
ipa dnszone-show 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa.
Add PTR records for IdM infrastructure
# Add PTR record for IdM server
ipa dnsrecord-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. 10 --ptr-rec=idm-server.example.com.

# Add PTR records for clients
ipa dnsrecord-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. 20 --ptr-rec=client1.example.com.
ipa dnsrecord-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. 21 --ptr-rec=client2.example.com.
Verify reverse DNS resolution
# Test reverse DNS lookup
dig @localhost -x 192.168.1.10
dig @localhost -x 192.168.1.20

# Using nslookup
nslookup 192.168.1.10 localhost
Subtask 1.4: Configure DNS Recursion and Security Settings
Configure DNS recursion settings
# Allow recursion for trusted networks
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-recursion=192.168.1.0/24
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-recursion=127.0.0.1

# Configure query sources
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-query=192.168.1.0/24
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-query=127.0.0.1
Set up DNS security options
# Enable DNS security extensions if needed
ipa dnsconfig-mod --allow-transfer=none

# Configure DNS update policy
ipa dnsconfig-show | grep -i policy
Task 2: Troubleshoot DNS Issues
Subtask 2.1: Use ipa dnszone-show for Diagnostics
The ipa dnszone-show command is essential for troubleshooting DNS issues in IdM.

Examine DNS zone details
# Show detailed information about the main zone
ipa dnszone-show example.com.

# Show all attributes including hidden ones
ipa dnszone-show example.com. --all

# Check reverse zone details
ipa dnszone-show 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. --all
Analyze DNS zone configuration
# Check zone serial numbers and SOA records
ipa dnsrecord-show example.com. @ --all

# Examine NS records
ipa dnsrecord-show example.com. @ --type=NS

# Check SRV records for IdM services
ipa dnsrecord-find example.com. --type=SRV
Subtask 2.2: Common DNS Troubleshooting Scenarios
Let's simulate and resolve common DNS issues.

Scenario 1: DNS Resolution Failure
# Simulate the issue by checking resolution
dig @localhost nonexistent.example.com

# Check DNS server logs
journalctl -u named-pkcs11 -f

# Verify DNS service is responding
systemctl status named-pkcs11
Scenario 2: Missing SRV Records
# Check for required IdM SRV records
dig @localhost _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV
dig @localhost _kerberos._tcp.example.com SRV
dig @localhost _kerberos._udp.example.com SRV

# If missing, recreate them
ipa dns-update-system-records --dry-run
ipa dns-update-system-records
Scenario 3: Incorrect PTR Records
# Test reverse DNS resolution
dig @localhost -x 192.168.1.10

# If incorrect, update the PTR record
ipa dnsrecord-mod 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. 10 --ptr-rec=idm-server.example.com.
Subtask 2.3: Advanced DNS Troubleshooting Tools
Use named-checkzone for zone validation
# Check zone file syntax (if accessible)
named-checkzone example.com /var/named/dyndb-ldap/ipa/example.com/

# Check configuration
named-checkconf
Monitor DNS queries in real-time
# Enable query logging
rndc querylog on

# Watch DNS queries
tail -f /var/log/messages | grep named

# Disable query logging when done
rndc querylog off
Test DNS from client perspective
# From client machines, test DNS resolution
ssh root@client1.example.com

# Test various record types
dig idm-server.example.com
dig _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV
dig example.com MX
Task 3: Validate DNS Resolution and Trust Establishment
Subtask 3.1: Comprehensive DNS Resolution Testing
Test forward DNS resolution
# Test A records for all hosts
for host in idm-server client1 client2; do
    echo "Testing $host.example.com:"
    dig @localhost $host.example.com A +short
done

# Test AAAA records if IPv6 is configured
dig @localhost idm-server.example.com AAAA
Test reverse DNS resolution
# Test PTR records for all IPs
for ip in 192.168.1.10 192.168.1.20 192.168.1.21; do
    echo "Testing reverse lookup for $ip:"
    dig @localhost -x $ip +short
done
Validate service discovery records
# Test SRV records for IdM services
echo "Testing LDAP SRV record:"
dig @localhost _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV

echo "Testing Kerberos SRV records:"
dig @localhost _kerberos._tcp.example.com SRV
dig @localhost _kerberos._udp.example.com SRV

echo "Testing Kerberos admin SRV record:"
dig @localhost _kerberos-adm._tcp.example.com SRV
Subtask 3.2: Validate Client DNS Configuration
Check client DNS settings
# Connect to client1
ssh root@client1.example.com

# Check DNS configuration
cat /etc/resolv.conf

# Verify it points to IdM server
# Should show: nameserver 192.168.1.10
Test DNS resolution from client
# Test basic resolution
nslookup idm-server.example.com

# Test service discovery
dig _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV

# Test reverse resolution
nslookup 192.168.1.10
Validate IdM client functionality
# Check IdM client status
ipa-client-install --unattended --no-ntp --force-join \
  --domain=example.com --server=idm-server.example.com \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM

# Test authentication
kinit admin
klist
Subtask 3.3: Trust Establishment Validation
Verify Kerberos realm trust
# Return to IdM server
ssh root@idm-server.example.com

# Check realm configuration
ipa config-show | grep -i realm

# Verify Kerberos configuration
cat /etc/krb5.conf
Test cross-realm authentication
# Test getting tickets for different services
kinit admin@EXAMPLE.COM
kvno host/client1.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM
kvno ldap/idm-server.example.com@EXAMPLE.COM
Validate trust relationships
# Check for any configured trusts
ipa trust-find

# Verify DNS delegation if trusts exist
ipa dnsforwardzone-find
Subtask 3.4: Performance and Monitoring Validation
Test DNS response times
# Create a simple DNS performance test script
cat > dns_performance_test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "DNS Performance Test Results:"
echo "============================="

for i in {1..10}; do
    start_time=$(date +%s%N)
    dig @localhost idm-server.example.com > /dev/null 2>&1
    end_time=$(date +%s%N)
    duration=$(( (end_time - start_time) / 1000000 ))
    echo "Query $i: ${duration}ms"
done
EOF

chmod +x dns_performance_test.sh
./dns_performance_test.sh
Monitor DNS service health
# Check DNS service statistics
rndc status

# View DNS service resource usage
systemctl status named-pkcs11 -l

# Check for any DNS-related errors
journalctl -u named-pkcs11 --since "1 hour ago" | grep -i error
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: DNS Service Won't Start
Symptoms: named-pkcs11 service fails to start

Solution:

# Check configuration syntax
named-checkconf

# Check SELinux contexts
restorecon -R /var/named/

# Check file permissions
ls -la /var/named/dyndb-ldap/

# Restart the service
systemctl restart named-pkcs11
Issue 2: External DNS Resolution Fails
Symptoms: Cannot resolve external domains

Solution:

# Check forwarder configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show | grep -i forward

# Test forwarder connectivity
dig @8.8.8.8 google.com

# Reconfigure forwarders if needed
ipa dnsconfig-mod --forwarder=8.8.8.8 --forwarder=1.1.1.1
Issue 3: Reverse DNS Not Working
Symptoms: PTR record queries fail

Solution:

# Verify reverse zone exists
ipa dnszone-find | grep in-addr.arpa

# Check PTR records
ipa dnsrecord-find 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa.

# Add missing PTR records
ipa dnsrecord-add 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa. 10 --ptr-rec=idm-server.example.com.
